WEBVTT


00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.460
Here's a hem or a trim that's
unique and cute as can be.

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:12.000
It's a little fussy, but not too bad and
there are some options for making it your

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:12.390
own.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.510
It's a double bed edge.

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:20.550
I found that a smooth yarn and that
shows stitch definition works best.

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.430
Set your machine for full needle rib.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.190
Your pitch will be H.

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:29.580
The tension on both beds
set it to about three.

00:00:30.735 --> 00:00:32.895
Set your machine for full needle rib.

00:00:34.035 --> 00:00:39.105
Now, even if you normally knit a zigzag,
row at zero, don't do that for this one.

00:00:39.135 --> 00:00:42.825
We're going to be manipulating the strands
and we need them to be a little bit loose,

00:00:42.825 --> 00:00:44.805
not sloppy, but loose.

00:00:46.115 --> 00:00:50.885
You'll want to have a multiple of three
plus two needles and work on the main bed.

00:00:51.805 --> 00:00:57.504
The corresponding needles in work on the
ribber, along with the two edge needles on

00:00:57.504 --> 00:00:58.105
the ribber.

00:01:00.055 --> 00:01:05.065
Knit the zigzag row, and carefully
hang the cast on comb and some weight.

00:01:05.905 --> 00:01:07.735
Don't knit a circular row.

00:01:10.585 --> 00:01:12.865
We'll transfer stitches as shown.

00:01:13.625 --> 00:01:16.205
You'll end up with three
stitches on one needle.

00:01:16.635 --> 00:01:21.105
And two empty needles, three stitches
on one needle, and two empty needles.

00:01:22.695 --> 00:01:27.195
I started by transferring the stitch
on the second needle on the left of the

00:01:27.195 --> 00:01:32.685
needle bed to its neighbor on the right,
and then transferred the next needle back

00:01:32.685 --> 00:01:36.585
to that same needle, putting
three stitches on each needle.

00:01:39.495 --> 00:01:43.275
This leaves an edge
stitch needle for seaming.

00:01:47.215 --> 00:01:52.080
Now work these transfers all the way
across the bed and make sure all of the

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:53.970
needles are in working position.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:57.270
Knit 4 rows.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:01:59.610
Everything should be knitting.

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:01.920
There are no needles and
out of work position.

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:08.610
Next, repeat the transfers over
the same needles in the same order.

00:02:24.095 --> 00:02:29.975
Make sure all of the needles are in work
again and continue knitting in full needle

00:02:40.175 --> 00:02:40.885
rib.

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:45.380
Can you see the pretty little eyelets
that are formed right along the top edge?

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:52.330
Now this would be a great edge for
a really easy full needle rib scarf.

00:02:58.075 --> 00:03:02.875
I don't know about you, but I don't often
knit garments and full needle rib, but we

00:03:02.875 --> 00:03:04.795
can still use this pretty scallop.

00:03:06.085 --> 00:03:11.305
After you work the two sets of transfers,
transfer the ribber needles up to the main

00:03:11.305 --> 00:03:11.725
bed,

00:03:14.455 --> 00:03:17.245
but you can continue just
knitting single bed from here.

00:03:17.894 --> 00:03:17.984
Okay.

00:03:24.185 --> 00:03:29.015
Another thing to notice is that the trim
is quite a bit wider than the stockinette,

00:03:29.644 --> 00:03:34.174
so you need to plan ahead a bit, cast on
your edge with fewer stitches than you

00:03:34.174 --> 00:03:39.245
need for your garment, and then increase
evenly before switching to stockinette.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:46.740
Now, having said that, I immediately
thought about the fact that tuck is wider

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:47.610
than stockinette.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:52.600
The tuck I chose is a
right side facing design.

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:58.810
In other words, the purlside is the right
side of the fabric to create the scallop

00:03:58.870 --> 00:03:59.260
edge.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:04.750
This time I reversed the technique and I
transferred the ribber stitches to form

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:05.530
the scallops.

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:12.150
And then transferred all the
ribber stitches up to the main bed.

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:17.700
This way, the public side of
the scallop trim was facing me.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:33.810
And as I work the tuck, the public
side of the tuck was also facing me.

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:40.910
The tuck stitch gave me more width and
took care of the flare that I found when I

00:04:40.910 --> 00:04:41.979
worked stockinette.

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:45.610
I hope you'll give this edge a try.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:49.780
There aren't too many steps and
transferring the stitches is well worth

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:49.900
it.

